ghpull:671 a bl:PullRequest, bl:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:label "sparql_endpoint: persist runtime-loaded rulesets across restart" ;
    bl:number 671 ;
    bl:state bl:Open ;
    bl:closesIssue ghissues:475 .

session:claudeSonnet5 a prov:SoftwareAgent ;
    rdfs:label "Claude Sonnet 5" .

session:pr671 a agp:AgentSession ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith session:claudeSonnet5 ;
    prov:used ghissues:475 ;
    prov:startedAtTime "2026-09-12T17:02:43Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-09-24T17:07:48Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

session:pr671Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Issue #475 was deferred from #390's plan doc: POST /{dataset}/rules (and #473's POST/DELETE /{dataset}/rules/{id}) mutate the live in-memory IncrementalReasoner but were never persisted, so a --data-dir restart loses any runtime-loaded ruleset. Reading pr-470/622/641's provenance summaries first established the existing model: DatasetEntry (registry.rs) holds a reasoner cell plus a rulesets: RulesetMap bookkeeping map, and all three HTTP handlers funnel through apply_combined_ruleset. The key design decision was what to persist: a Rule's QuadPattern holds interned GraphElementId values that are not stable across a restart (a fresh GraphElementManager assigns ids in whatever order data is reloaded), exactly the problem the existing quad changelog already solves for quads via the portable ElementRepr. The equivalent portable form for a ruleset mutation turned out to be simpler than for quads: just the raw Datalog source text, since datalog_parser::parse already interns IRIs into whatever store it is given. Three new LogEntry variants (ReplaceRuleset/SetNamedRuleset/DeleteNamedRuleset) were added to the SAME redb table/sequence the quad log already uses (not a separate table), so relative ordering between id-scoped set/delete operations replays correctly. The three rules_endpoint.rs handlers were refactored to share their apply-and-bookkeeping core (apply_replace_ruleset_text/apply_set_named_ruleset_text/apply_delete_named_ruleset, returning a RulesetApplyError distinguishing Parse from Conflict for correct 400 vs 409 mapping) with a new startup replay path added to serve_on_listener, run after quad-changelog replay and after Config::initial_rules builds the initial reasoner -- so a persisted runtime ruleset change correctly overrides a --rules-supplied one on restart, mirroring live replace semantics. QuadChangelog::compact was found to silently discard any ruleset log entries mixed into the same table (it rewrites the whole table as a live-quad-only snapshot) and was fixed to read back and re-append ruleset entries unchanged. TDD: 7 new integration tests in tests/persist_rulesets.rs (full replace, replace-then-replace-again ordering, named ruleset, named ruleset delete, empty-body unload, no-data-dir is memory-only, and a compact-then-restart regression test) all written first, confirmed red, then made green together. A workspace-wide cargo test --workspace run showed 4 apparently-flaky failures in the new test file and one missing test binary; both were confirmed to be the documented shared-CARGO_TARGET_DIR hazard from concurrent worktree builds (another worktree was mid-build at the same moment) by re-running the same tests in isolation, where they passed reliably every time -- no code changes were needed to fix them. CI on PR #671 came back fully green except Security Audit, which was confirmed (via a fresh CI run on main itself) to fail identically on main due to RUSTSEC-2026-0285 (rustls), unrelated to this PR's unchanged Cargo.lock -- filed as issue #678 rather than scope-creeping this PR into a dependency bump." ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:671 ;
    agp:decisionPoint session:pr671Decision ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr671 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .

session:pr671Decision a agp:Decision ;
    agp:summaryText "Logged each ruleset-endpoint changelog entry only AFTER apply_combined_ruleset (and, for id-scoped ops, the rulesets bookkeeping update) succeeded, immediately before building the 200 response -- not before, unlike the quad-mutation changelog path in sparql_update.rs." ;
    agp:alternative "Considered mirroring sparql_update.rs's log-before-apply ordering exactly (log, then mutate in-memory state), for the same crash-durability reasoning quads use. Rejected: the quad path only ever logs an ALREADY-validated operation (prepare_update returns Result and only the Ok path reaches the log-then-apply step), but a rules-endpoint request has no clean validate/apply split -- datalog_parser::parse interns IRIs into the store as a side effect, and whether the combined ruleset is even acceptable (not contradictory) is only known after apply_combined_ruleset actually runs. Logging before that point risks durably recording an operation that never actually took effect live (a parse error or 409 conflict), which replay would then need to silently reconcile. Logging only on confirmed success keeps the invariant simple: every entry in the log is exactly and only an operation that really happened, at the cost of a narrow window where a changelog-append I/O failure after a successful in-memory apply leaves memory ahead of the durable log for that one operation -- documented in the plan doc as an accepted, pre-existing-in-spirit risk rather than something this PR needed to solve." .
